WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.111 --> 00:00:09.234
<v Maya>Picture this. You're scrolling through your feed on a Tuesday morning and you see a headline. It says, drinking three cups of coffee a day is associated with living longer.</v>

2
00:00:09.654 --> 00:00:10.734
<v Daniel>Oh, I see those all the time.</v>

3
00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:20.777
<v Maya>Right. Now, be completely honest with yourself. When you read that, do you instantly think, oh, great, coffee causes a longer life. I should probably start drinking more.</v>

4
00:00:21.165 --> 00:00:28.841
<v Daniel>I mean, yeah, most of us make that leap instantly. We see two things happening together and our brains just, you know, immediately construct a story connecting them.</v>

5
00:00:29.101 --> 00:00:31.246
<v Maya>Exactly. Coffee equals a longer life.</v>

6
00:00:31.692 --> 00:00:33.152
<v Daniel>Yeah, we want the world to have clear rules.</v>

7
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:39.934
<v Maya>But in the world of science, we are constantly beaten over the head with the golden rule, right? Correlation does not imply causation.</v>

8
00:00:40.115 --> 00:00:44.436
<v Daniel>Right. Just because two things happen at the same time doesn't mean one caused the other.</v>

9
00:00:44.536 --> 00:00:58.420
<v Maya>Exactly. Yet when we read about a new study, we almost always make that leap anyway. And today we are going to look at why that happens. And more shockingly, who is actually encouraging us to make that leap?</v>

10
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:04.343
<v Daniel>Because it turns out we can't just blame the media or casual readers for misunderstanding the science.</v>

11
00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:04.943
<v Maya>No, we can't.</v>

12
00:01:05.343 --> 00:01:08.565
<v Daniel>A massive amount of the time, it is the scientists themselves baiting the hook.</v>

13
00:01:08.758 --> 00:01:23.601
<v Maya>Yeah. And that brings us to what we're doing today. Today's deep dive is unpacking this monumental new paper. It was published in Nature Human Behavior on August 24th, 2026. I knew. Super recent. It's by Calvin Ish, Timothy</v>

14
00:01:23.641 --> 00:01:34.604
<v Maya>Doerr, Neil Fashing, Grace Jennings, and Duncan J. Watts out of the University of Pennsylvania. And our mission today is to explore a phenomenon they call narrative license.</v>

15
00:01:35.004 --> 00:01:36.825
<v Daniel>narrative license. I love that term. It's</v>

16
00:01:36.886 --> 00:01:50.976
<v Maya>so good. We are looking at how social scientists frequently use language that wildly overreaches their actual statistical proof and how artificial intelligence is simultaneously highlighting this problem and in many ways actively making it worse.</v>

17
00:01:51.236 --> 00:02:05.947
<v Daniel>Yeah, and to really ground this, we are stacking this new paper with four background studies. Because we need to understand the underlying psychology of, well, why humans fall for this. Right. And the systemic pressures that force scientists to do it. Plus, the cutting-edge AI methods required to</v>

18
00:02:06.087 --> 00:02:07.989
<v Daniel>even measure a problem of this scale.</v>

19
00:02:08.307 --> 00:02:16.212
<v Maya>Okay, let's unpack this. Before we can judge the impact of scientists exaggerating their claims, we have to understand the sheer scale of the problem.</v>

20
00:02:16.272 --> 00:02:17.052
<v Daniel>Which is massive.</v>

21
00:02:17.193 --> 00:02:22.236
<v Maya>It really is. And to do that, we have to define what a cross-sectional study actually is.</v>

22
00:02:22.516 --> 00:02:25.218
<v Daniel>Right, so a cross-sectional study is essentially a photograph.</v>

23
00:02:25.318 --> 00:02:25.598
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

24
00:02:25.958 --> 00:02:39.862
<v Daniel>You were looking at data from a single point in time across a specific population. Because you aren't tracking variables over time like a video or running a controlled experiment where you manipulate a variable, you could only show an association.</v>

25
00:02:40.022 --> 00:02:42.703
<v Maya>You can just prove that two things are happening simultaneously.</v>

26
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:49.325
<v Daniel>Exactly. By definition, the design cannot establish causation. You absolutely cannot say X made Y happen.</v>

27
00:02:49.485 --> 00:02:57.331
<v Maya>Yet when the authors of this new paper analyzed nearly 200,000 cross-sectional articles, they found something deeply alarming.</v>

28
00:02:57.432 --> 00:02:58.733
<v Daniel>Yeah, the numbers are wild.</v>

29
00:02:58.973 --> 00:03:07.760
<v Maya>Across 194,631 cross-sectional articles, 46.3% use causal language in the title or the abstract.</v>

30
00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:08.361
<v Daniel>Almost half.</v>

31
00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:15.446
<v Maya>Almost half. And the historical trend is what really gets me here. From 1980 to the year 2000, that number hovered around 20%.</v>

32
00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.571
<v Daniel>Which is still a lot, but, you know, somewhat stable.</v>

33
00:03:19.591 --> 00:03:25.073
<v Maya>Right. But then it skyrocketed. It rose almost threefold to over 60% by 2024.</v>

34
00:03:25.454 --> 00:03:36.058
<v Daniel>Which means that right now, if you are reading a cross-sectional social science paper, it is more likely than not that the authors are claiming a causal relationship their data simply cannot prove.</v>

35
00:03:36.158 --> 00:03:39.519
<v Maya>Which is, I mean, is this basically the academic version of clickbait?</v>

36
00:03:39.559 --> 00:03:43.681
<v Daniel>Honestly, yes. It's dressing up an association to sound like a hard fact.</v>

37
00:03:44.208 --> 00:03:53.495
<v Maya>Okay, but I'm stuck on the logistics here. How on earth does a human research team read almost 200,000 dense academic papers without spending 30 years doing it?</v>

38
00:03:53.635 --> 00:04:02.442
<v Daniel>Well, they didn't. They had to use a very modern three-step human-in-the-loop workflow inside a platform called ProQuest's TDM Studio.</v>

39
00:04:02.462 --> 00:04:03.303
<v Maya>TDM Studio.</v>

40
00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:09.446
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's what's known as a walled garden environment. Publishers keep their data strictly locked down, right? So you can't just scrape the internet for them.</v>

41
00:04:09.466 --> 00:04:10.846
<v Maya>Oh, because of copyrights and all that.</v>

42
00:04:10.926 --> 00:04:18.668
<v Daniel>Exactly. So first, they ran a massive keyword search within that walled garden, which returned 321,322 candidate studies.</v>

43
00:04:18.928 --> 00:04:20.328
<v Maya>Still way too many for a human to read.</v>

44
00:04:20.608 --> 00:04:27.410
<v Daniel>Way too many. So step two, they took a small sample, just 200 abstracts, and had human experts code them.</v>

45
00:04:27.799 --> 00:04:29.861
<v Maya>OK, humans doing the baseline work.</v>

46
00:04:30.001 --> 00:04:42.752
<v Daniel>Right. And they use those to validate a prompt for GPT-4-0, asking the AI to classify if a study was truly cross-sectional. Once that was validated, they unleashed GPT-4-0 on 30,000 abstracts.</v>

47
00:04:43.956 --> 00:04:49.661
<v Maya>Wait, if GPT-4 is so smart, why didn't they just have it read all 300,000 papers? Why stop at 30,000?</v>

48
00:04:50.001 --> 00:04:58.908
<v Daniel>Economics and scale, mostly. Running massive, large language models on hundreds of thousands of academic texts gets incredibly expensive. And computationally heavy.</v>

49
00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:00.660
<v Maya>Ah, right. It burns through tokens.</v>

50
00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:05.643
<v Daniel>Exactly. Yeah. So they distilled the knowledge GPT-4 generated into a fine-tuned BERT classifier.</v>

51
00:05:05.783 --> 00:05:06.263
<v Maya>Okay, BERT.</v>

52
00:05:06.603 --> 00:05:16.809
<v Daniel>Yeah, BERT is an older, lighter, more specialized AI model that is exceptionally good at text classification. They trained BERT on the 30,000 GPT-labeled abstracts and then validated it on another 10,000.</v>

53
00:05:16.869 --> 00:05:20.851
<v Maya>Did it actually work, though? I mean, going from human to GPT to BERT feels like a game of telephone.</v>

54
00:05:21.031 --> 00:05:25.554
<v Daniel>It did work. It achieved an F1 score of 0.92 and an overall accuracy of 0.91.</v>

55
00:05:26.274 --> 00:05:36.949
<v Maya>Let's break down that F1 score for a second, because we see that term thrown around a lot in AI. And I think people get confused if accuracy is just, you know, how many did you get right? What is F1 measuring?</v>

56
00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:45.043
<v Daniel>Think of it like a brutal exam that tests two things at once. It tests your ability to find all the needles in a haystack. That's recall.</v>

57
00:05:45.203 --> 00:05:46.023
<v Maya>Okay, recall.</v>

58
00:05:46.223 --> 00:05:51.785
<v Daniel>But it also tests your ability to not accidentally pick up pieces of hay and call them needles. That's precision.</v>

59
00:05:52.025 --> 00:05:52.705
<v Maya>Oh, that makes sense.</v>

60
00:05:52.865 --> 00:06:06.810
<v Daniel>So an F1 score of .92 means BERT was incredibly good at finding the cross-sectional studies without getting confused by false positives. And once they had that dialed in, they used a parallel BERT classifier to hunt for the causal language itself.</v>

61
00:06:07.001 --> 00:06:12.666
<v Maya>And we have a background paper from Fabrizio Gilardi and colleagues in 2023 that really puts this into perspective.</v>

62
00:06:12.926 --> 00:06:13.807
<v Daniel>The annotation paper.</v>

63
00:06:13.947 --> 00:06:26.038
<v Maya>Yeah. Gilardi proved that ChatGPT could beat human crowd workers on text annotation tasks by about 25 points. But the kicker is the cost. It did it for under a third of a cent per annotation.</v>

64
00:06:26.438 --> 00:06:39.609
<v Daniel>Which is just, that economic shift is profound. Analyzing 194,631 articles wasn't just computationally possible, it was dirt cheap. But we have to call out the elephant in the room here.</v>

65
00:06:39.709 --> 00:06:41.090
<v Maya>The reflexivity paradox.</v>

66
00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:41.610
<v Daniel>Yes.</v>

67
00:06:41.931 --> 00:06:53.500
<v Maya>The irony is almost too much to handle. The authors are using large language models as the very instrument to measure a problem exaggerated scientific claims that LLMs actually make much worse, which we will definitely get into later.</v>

68
00:06:53.700 --> 00:07:02.282
<v Daniel>It's a fascinating loop, using the disease to diagnose the disease. But let's look at the data they extracted. Who are the worst offenders when it comes to overreaching?</v>

69
00:07:02.462 --> 00:07:04.283
<v Maya>The disciplinary breakdown is wild.</v>

70
00:07:04.723 --> 00:07:05.003
<v Daniel>Yeah.</v>

71
00:07:05.123 --> 00:07:11.705
<v Maya>Business is the absolute highest. A staggering 84% of cross-sectional business papers use causal language.</v>

72
00:07:11.987 --> 00:07:13.589
<v Daniel>Wow. 84%. Yeah.</v>

73
00:07:13.749 --> 00:07:22.117
<v Maya>Then economics at 67%, psychology at 55%, political science at 53%, and sociology is the lowest at 46%. I mean,</v>

74
00:07:22.457 --> 00:07:37.307
<v Daniel>that makes total sense if you think about the audience for those papers. Well, an executive or a policymaker reading a business or economics paper... doesn't want to hear, oh, these two factors interestingly co-occur. They want to know, what lever do I pull to make</v>

75
00:07:37.347 --> 00:07:38.427
<v Daniel>my profit margin go up?</v>

76
00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:42.749
<v Maya>They want actionable results, even if the math doesn't actually support the action.</v>

77
00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:43.369
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

78
00:07:43.609 --> 00:07:52.352
<v Maya>And here's where it gets really interesting. You might assume this is a problem with lower tier, maybe predatory journals that don't have rigorous peer review.</v>

79
00:07:52.512 --> 00:07:53.973
<v Daniel>That would be the logical guess, yeah. But</v>

80
00:07:54.093 --> 00:08:09.080
<v Maya>the Ish paper found the exact opposite. Higher impact journals are actually more likely to overreach. Really? Yeah. In journals with an impact factor above 3.03, causal language sits at 54.4%. For journals below</v>

81
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.642
<v Maya>the median impact factor, it drops to 43.3%.</v>

82
00:08:12.623 --> 00:08:16.166
<v Daniel>So the more prestigious the journal, the more likely the authors are to claim causation.</v>

83
00:08:16.186 --> 00:08:30.920
<v Maya>Yep. They even looked at a very specific journal, the Journal of Quantitative Description, Digital Media. Okay. This journal explicitly bans causal claims in its submissions. They practically have a giant neon sign on their submission page saying, no causation allowed.</v>

84
00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:39.548
<v Maya>And yet the researchers still found a 12.9% rate of causal language. It's like a vegan restaurant getting caught serving bacon in 13% of its dishes.</v>

85
00:08:40.952 --> 00:08:50.776
<v Daniel>That is hilarious, but also terrible. It shows how deeply ingrained this habit is. And if you think the abstracts are bad, wait until you look at the full texts.</v>

86
00:08:51.056 --> 00:08:53.537
<v Maya>Oh, right. The BERT classifier ran on the full text, too.</v>

87
00:08:53.557 --> 00:09:05.302
<v Daniel>Yeah. When the researchers unleashed their AI on the bodies of these articles, they found that 81.8% of the articles contained at least one unhedged causal snippet somewhere in the paper.</v>

88
00:09:05.666 --> 00:09:19.135
<v Maya>Even when the authors were on their best behavior in the abstract, let's say you read an abstract that is purely descriptive, incredibly careful, no big claims. If you read the full text, 44% of the time there is an unhedged, direct, causal claim buried in there.</v>

89
00:09:19.176 --> 00:09:20.296
<v Daniel>Just hiding in the paragraphs.</v>

90
00:09:20.416 --> 00:09:29.763
<v Maya>Yeah, and if the abstract used implied or conditional language-like, this might lead to never 72% of the time they drop the act in the full text and just go full causal.</v>

91
00:09:30.188 --> 00:09:43.785
<v Daniel>And if we map out where these claims hide, a very clear pattern emerges. These unhedged claims cluster heavily in the introduction, 60% of the time, and the discussion section, 55.1% of the time.</v>

92
00:09:45.293 --> 00:09:46.554
<v Maya>But in the methods section.</v>

93
00:09:46.634 --> 00:09:47.414
<v Daniel>Only 6.1%.</v>

94
00:09:47.814 --> 00:09:55.278
<v Maya>It's like they're strict accountants when they are doing the math in the methods section, but they turn into marketing executives the second they get to the discussion section. That</v>

95
00:09:55.298 --> 00:10:00.860
<v Daniel>is exactly what's fascinating here. They know the rules. They just choose to break them to sell the narrative.</v>

96
00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:07.702
<v Maya>Which brings us to a vital question. Does this narrative license actually work? Like, does it fool anyone?</v>

97
00:10:07.842 --> 00:10:15.927
<v Daniel>Right. Because if no one believes it, it doesn't matter. But to find out, the researchers ran a pre-registered experiment on human readers.</v>

98
00:10:16.247 --> 00:10:26.192
<v Maya>Yeah, they recruited 11,105 U.S. adults on a platform called Prolific. And crucially, all of them had at least a bachelor's degree. So we are talking about an educated audience. Highly</v>

99
00:10:26.272 --> 00:10:26.692
<v Daniel>educated.</v>

100
00:10:26.772 --> 00:10:31.915
<v Maya>They gave them one of 28 abstracts from elite journals to read under four different conditions.</v>

101
00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:43.209
<v Daniel>So Condition 1 was the original abstract, causal language intact. Condition 2, a rewritten version, scrubbed completely clean into strict dissociational language.</v>

102
00:10:43.589 --> 00:10:44.890
<v Maya>No causal claims at all.</v>

103
00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:57.861
<v Daniel>Right. Condition 3 was the original abstract, but with a warning note about the methodological limits. And Condition 4 was the warning note, plus an AI feedback label literally highlighting the overreach in the text.</v>

104
00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:06.663
<v Maya>And the interventions did work to a degree, but we really need to look at the numbers here. They measured the drop in belief using a beta coefficient.</v>

105
00:11:07.023 --> 00:11:19.668
<v Daniel>Okay, think of the beta coefficient like a volume dial on a stereo. A beta of zero means the intervention had no effect on the reader's belief. A negative beta means it turned the volume down on their belief that the study proved causation.</v>

106
00:11:19.948 --> 00:11:25.750
<v Maya>So the methodological label had a beta of metasor 0.4 and the associational rewrites had a beta of netasor 0.3.</v>

107
00:11:26.397 --> 00:11:30.638
<v Daniel>Okay, so scrubbing language or adding a warning label did turn the dial down. People were more skeptical.</v>

108
00:11:30.738 --> 00:11:35.220
<v Maya>They were, but here's the core insight, the part that genuinely blew my mind, the residue. The</v>

109
00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:36.040
<v Daniel>causal residue.</v>

110
00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:43.142
<v Maya>Yes. Even when causal claims were explicitly denied or entirely absent, readers kept inferring them.</v>

111
00:11:43.758 --> 00:11:48.820
