<v Maya>Oh, we can't.</v>

157
00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:42.904
<v Daniel>We have to invite you, the listener, to just sit with that contradiction. We are in a transitional moment where we don't fully understand if the technology is naturally correcting itself or if we are just playing a game of whack-a-mole with different prompts and different updates.</v>

158
00:16:43.084 --> 00:16:57.276
<v Maya>But it brings us back to the systemic why. Both humans and AI are eager to strip out the hedges, the mites, and maybes. But why do the scientists initiate this chain reaction? Why write overreaching claims in the first place? It's not because they are, you know,</v>

159
00:16:57.637 --> 00:16:59.979
<v Maya>mustache-twirling villains trying to trick the public.</v>

160
00:17:00.319 --> 00:17:10.548
<v Daniel>No, it's rarely conscious cheating. To understand this, we look at Paul Smaldino and Richard McGillreath's 2016 formal model of the natural selection of bad science.</v>

161
00:17:10.870 --> 00:17:12.491
<v Maya>Natural selection of bad science.</v>

162
00:17:12.531 --> 00:17:19.734
<v Daniel>Yeah. It explains that threefold rise in over-claiming since the year 2000, better than any individual bad actor story could.</v>

163
00:17:20.434 --> 00:17:30.559
<v Maya>Because if you think about the institutional incentives hiring committees, promotion boards, and grant funders, they all emphasize one thing above all else, right? Impact.</v>

164
00:17:30.659 --> 00:17:34.261
<v Daniel>You need publications. And journals select for novelty and importance.</v>

165
00:17:34.548 --> 00:17:45.994
<v Maya>So over time, these systemic pressures naturally select for bad methods and overreaching language. The scientists who hedge their bets and write incredibly cautious dry papers don't get the cover of nature.</v>

166
00:17:46.395 --> 00:17:52.038
<v Daniel>No, they don't get 10 years fast either. The ones who write the bold causal claims do. It's an evolutionary pressure.</v>

167
00:17:52.339 --> 00:17:58.325
<v Maya>And if we map that out, we see what Tal Yarkoni called the generalizability crisis in his 2022 paper.</v>

168
00:17:58.505 --> 00:18:06.573
<v Daniel>Right. He points out that there is a massive unacknowledged gap between the strict statistical models researchers use and the sweeping verbal hypotheses they write.</v>

169
00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:21.076
<v Maya>The math might prove that in a very specific lab setting with 50 undergrads, variable A moves slightly when variable B moved. But the verbal conclusion in the discussion section claims they have uncovered a fundamental truth about human psychology.</v>

170
00:18:21.236 --> 00:18:32.601
<v Daniel>It's like a car manufacturer testing a vehicle's brakes at 10 miles an hour in an empty parking lot. That's the strict math. But then putting up a billboard claiming the car can safely stop on a dime during a hurricane.</v>

171
00:18:32.788 --> 00:18:39.890
<v Maya>The math doesn't match the marketing at all. But, I mean, should we just force every scientist to write like a robot, completely devoid of all narrative? Well,</v>

172
00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:51.012
<v Daniel>Ish and his team are actually very careful to balance the argument here. Over-claiming isn't always a disaster. Sometimes it accelerates scientific progress by prompting fierce debate and rapid follow-up studies.</v>

173
00:18:51.212 --> 00:18:53.953
<v Maya>They mentioned Ronald Fisher, the famous statistician, right?</v>

174
00:18:53.973 --> 00:19:06.899
<v Daniel>Yes. Fisher was notoriously skeptical about the link between smoking and cancer. Right. Why? Because the evidence was observational. It was cross-sectional and correlational. Technically, his skepticism was rooted in solid methodology.</v>

175
00:19:07.259 --> 00:19:13.865
<v Maya>But obviously, practically, waiting for a randomized controlled trial on smoking would have been a public health disaster.</v>

176
00:19:13.965 --> 00:19:16.888
<v Daniel>Exactly. Sometimes you have to make the leap based on association.</v>

177
00:19:17.449 --> 00:19:28.039
<v Maya>And beyond that, the authors argue that eliminating narrative entirely would be counterproductive. Humans are storytelling creatures. We need a narrative to understand and retain information.</v>

178
00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:35.286
<v Daniel>It's a tightrope walk. You need the story to make people care. But if the story strays too far from the mass, you're just writing fiction.</v>

179
00:19:35.787 --> 00:19:41.933
<v Maya>Which brings us to the ultimate paradox of this entire deep dive, the artificial intelligence paradox.</v>

180
00:19:42.545 --> 00:19:55.376
<v Daniel>The very same technology, the large language models, that eagerly strips the hedges out of scientific text, flattening nuance into clickbait, is the exact tool the authors propose using to flag and fix this overreach at scale.</v>

181
00:19:55.656 --> 00:20:10.222
<v Maya>And it all comes down to the power of the prompt. Just remind yourself of that final data point from the machine experiments. When Isha's team used a careful prompt, one that explicitly asked the AI to focus on methodological limitations... the causal overreach rate plummeted.</v>

182
00:20:10.362 --> 00:20:13.844
<v Daniel>It dropped down to just 75 causal summaries out of 500.</v>

183
00:20:14.264 --> 00:20:16.525
<v Maya>It cut the overreach down to roughly 5%.</v>

184
00:20:17.466 --> 00:20:24.630
<v Daniel>A single sentence in a prompt completely changed how the machine translated the truth, which leaves us with a provocative thought to mull over.</v>

185
00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:25.250
<v Maya>Let's hear it.</v>

186
00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:40.304
<v Daniel>We live in a world of information overload. More and more people are consuming research, not by reading the methodology of a 30-page PDF, but by asking an AI to summarize it for them. If so much of the credibility of global</v>

187
00:20:40.344 --> 00:20:55.316
<v Daniel>scientific literature now rides on a single AI prompt, what happens as more readers meet research through an automated summary they didn't explicitly ask to be careful? When you read an AI-generated brief for a meeting, who decides what nuance gets</v>

188
00:20:55.336 --> 00:20:55.897
<v Daniel>stripped away?</v>

189
00:20:56.246 --> 00:21:11.119
<v Maya>When you ask for the simple answer, the AI is more than happy to give you a definitive cause and effect, even if the science doesn't back it up. You might think you're getting the cold, hard facts, but you're just getting a story perfectly tailored to what your brain naturally wants to hear. Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.</v>

190
00:21:11.379 --> 00:21:17.893
<v Maya>Keep questioning the narratives you read, and the next time you see a headline about coffee, maybe remember the parking lot and the hurricane.</v>
